          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em abc conjecture
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em ABC conjecture
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/number+theory"} number theoretic
            conjecture due to (
            %a{:href => "#Oesterl&#233;Masser"}> Oesterlé-Masser 1985
            ), which says that there are only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finitely many
            integer solutions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation"} equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi for
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a + b = c   \;\;\; for \;\;\;  a,b,c \geq 1 \in \mathbb{N}
          %p
            (or instead
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo +
                    %mi b
                    %mo +
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a+b+c= 0
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer+numbers"} integer numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c
            to have no common factor as well as having “joint power” greater than a given bound.
          %p
            Here the
            %em power
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b,c
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi log
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %mi log
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi rad
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P(a,b,c) \coloneqq \frac{log|c|}{log(rad(a\cdot b\cdot c))}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radical"} radical
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi rad
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} rad(n)
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is the product of all its distinct
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prime+factors"} prime factors
          %p The precise form of the conjecture is:
          .un_theorem
            %h6#conjecture Conjecture
            %p
              %strong (abc conjecture)
            %p
              For any number
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon\gt 0
              there are only finitely many positive relatively prime (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coprime+integers"}> coprime
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
              solutions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b,c)
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation"} equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo +
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a + b = c
              with power
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi ϵ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(a,b,c)\geq 1+\epsilon
          %p
            According to (
            %a{:href => "#Matzur"}> Mazur
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p The beauty of such a Conjecture is that it captures the intuitive sense that triples of numbers which satisfy a linear relation, and which are divisible by high perfect powers, are rare; the precision of the Conjecture goads one to investigate this rarity quantitatively. Its very statement makes an attractive appeal to perform a range of numerical experiments that would test the empirical waters. On a theoretical level, it is enlightening to understand its relationship to the constellation of standard arithmetic theorems, conjectures, questions, etc., and we shall give some indications of this below.
          %h2#masons_theorem Mason’s Theorem
          %p
            According to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lang"} Lang
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in k[t]
            over an algebraically closed field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of characteristic
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi n
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_0(f)
            to be the number of
            %em distinct
            roots of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            %em without
            regard to multiplicity.
          .un_theorem
            %h6#theorem_mason
              Theorem (
              %a{:href => "#Mason"}> Mason
              )
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b, c \in k[t]
              be relatively prime polynomials, not all constant, such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a + b = c
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \max \{deg(a), deg(b), deg(c)\} \leq n_0(a b c) - 1
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              Let
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = a/c
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g = b/c
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo +
                      %mi g
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f + g = 1
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ′
                      %mi f
                    %mi f
                    %mo +
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ′
                      %mi g
                    %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{f'}{f} f + \frac{g'}{g} g = 0
            %p whence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi f
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi g
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b/a = g/f = -\frac{f'/f}{g'/g}.
            %p Put
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi m
                          %mi i
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi j
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi n
                          %mi j
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi k
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi k
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a(t) = c_1 \prod (t - \alpha_i)^{m_i}, \qquad b(t) = c_2 \prod (t - \beta_j)^{n_j}, \qquad c(t) = c_3 \prod (t - \gamma_k)^{p_k}.
            %p Then
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi b
                      %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mi g
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi g
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi m
                              %mi i
                          %mrow
                            %mi t
                            %mo −
                            %msub
                              %mi α
                              %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mi k
                          %mrow
                            %mi t
                            %mo −
                            %msub
                              %mi γ
                              %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi n
                              %mi j
                          %mrow
                            %mi t
                            %mo −
                            %msub
                              %mi β
                              %mi j
                        %mo −
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mfrac
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mi k
                          %mrow
                            %mi t
                            %mo −
                            %msub
                              %mi γ
                              %mi k
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{b}{a} = -\frac{f'/f}{g'/g} = -\frac{\sum \frac{m_i}{t - \alpha_i} - \sum \frac{p_k}{t - \gamma_k}}{\sum \frac{n_j}{t - \beta_j} - \sum \frac{p_k}{t - \gamma_k}} .
            %p
              A common denominator for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f'/f
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g'/g
              is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 0
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi β
                      %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi γ
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_0 = \prod (t - \alpha_i) \prod (t - \beta_j) \prod (t - \gamma_k)
            %p
              whose degree is
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n_0(a b c)
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mfrac
                      %mi b
                      %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mn 0
                        %mi f
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi N
                          %mn 0
                        %mi g
                        %mo ′
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{b}{a} = -\frac{N_0 f'/f}{N_0 g'/g}
            %p
              where the numerator and denominator on the right are polynomials. However, since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              are relatively prime, the fraction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b/a
              is already in lowest terms. From this we conclude that
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mn 0
                      %mi f
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg(b) \leq deg(N_0 f'/f) \leq n_0(a b c) - 1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mn 0
                      %mi g
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi n
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} deg(a) \leq deg(N_0 g'/g) \leq n_0(a b c) - 1
          .un_cor
            %h6#corollary_flt_for_polynomials Corollary (FLT for polynomials)
            %p
              Assume
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y, z \in k[t]
              are relatively prime polynomials, not all constant, and suppose
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %msup
                        %mi y
                        %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^n + y^n = z^n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≤
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \leq 2
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              From Mason’s theorem, we conclude
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mi deg
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi z
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n deg(x) = deg(x^n) \leq deg(x) + deg(y) + deg(z) - 1
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
              by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
              on the left. Adding the results, we have
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi deg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi deg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi deg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi deg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi deg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo +
                    %mi deg
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo −
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n(deg(x) + deg(y) + deg(z)) \leq 3(deg(x) + deg(y) + deg(z)) - 3
            %p
              which is impossible if
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 3
          %p
            Guided by analogies between the ring of integers and the ring of polynomials in one variable, and building on insights of Mason, Frey, Szpiro, and others, Masser and Oesterlé were led to formulate the abc conjecture for integers as follows. Again define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi N
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_0(m)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            a non-zero integer to be the number of distinct primes dividing
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
          %ul
            %li
              Conjecture: For all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon \lt 0
              there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(\epsilon) \lt 0
              such that for relatively prime integers
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b, c
              satisfying
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo +
                      %mi b
                      %mo =
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a + b = c
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi max
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ,
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi b
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ,
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                        %mi c
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi N
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mi b
                      %mi c
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo +
                          %mi ϵ
                      %mo .
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \max({|a|}, {|b|}, {|c|}) \leq C(\epsilon)N_0(a b c)^{1+\epsilon}.
          %p
            Of course, this differs from the polynomial case because of the presence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo +
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1+ \epsilon
            in the exponent, but this is necessary. For example, for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \gt 0
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a + b = c
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mi C
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mi b
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {|a|} \gt C N_0(a b c)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mn 3
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mn 2
                          %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = 3^{2^n}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b = -1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo −
                  %msup
                    %mi y
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2 - y^2 = (x-y)(x+y)
            that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo +
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = a + b
            is of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mn 2
                    %mi n
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2^n d
            for some integer
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            sufficiently large, we can easily derive the claimed inequality.
          %h2#relation_to_other_statements Relation to other statements
          %p
            The abc conjecture implies the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordell+conjecture"} Mordell conjecture
            (
            %a{:href => "#Elkies"}> Elkies
            ).
          %p
            It is equivalent to the general form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Szpiro%27s+conjecture"} Szpiro's conjecture
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vojta%27s+conjecture"} Vojta's conjecture
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The abc conjecture was stated in
          %ul
            %li{:id => "Oesterl&#233;Masser"} Joseph Oesterlé, David Masser (1985)
          %p Mason’s theorem was presented in
          %ul
            %li#Mason
              R. C. Mason, Equations over function fields. In
              = succeed "," do
                %em Number Theory, Proceedings of the Noordwijkerhout
          %p Material on Mason’s theorem and its relation to the abc conjecture was taken from
          %ul
            %li#Lang
              Serge Lang, Algebra (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mn 3
                      %mi rd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 3^{rd}
              Edition), Addison-Wesley (1993), 194-196.
          %p
            The relation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mordell+conjecture"} Mordell conjecture
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Elkies
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Noam Elkies
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Noam+Elkies"} ?
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em ABC conjecture implies Mordell
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Szpiro%27s+conjecture"} Szpiro's conjecture
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Matt Baker (notes taken by William Stein),
              %em Elliptic curves, the ABC conjecture, and points of small canonical height
              (
              %a{:href => "http://modular.math.washington.edu/mcs/archive/Fall2001/notes/12-10-01/12-10-01.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p See also
          %ul#Mazur
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Barry+Mazur"}> Barry Mazur
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Questions about number
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~mazur/papers/scanQuest.pdf"} pdf
              scan
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                PolyMath,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://michaelnielsen.org/polymath1/index.php?title=ABC_conjecture"} ABC conjecture
            %li
              %p
                Abderrahmane Nitaj,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://www.math.unicaen.fr/~nitaj/abc.html"} The ABC Conjecture Homepage
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abc_conjecture"} abc conjecture
          %h3#alleged_proof_via_iut Alleged proof via IUT
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Shinichi+Mochizuki"} Shinichi Mochizuki
            anounced the proof which the mathematical community perceives as a serious but unchecked claim. See the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inter-universal+Teichm%C3%BCller+theory"} inter-universal Teichmüller theory
          %p Comments on the proof are at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mochizuki%27s+proof+of+abc"} Mochizuki's proof of abc
                \.
            %li
              %p
                MathOverflow,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/106560/philosophy-behind-mochizukis-work-on-the-abc-conjecture/106658#106658"} Philosophy behind Mochizuki’s work on the ABC conjecture
          %p A popular account of the problem of the math community checking the proof is in
          %ul
            %li
              Caroline Chen,
              %em The Paradox of the Proof
              (
              %a{:href => "http://projectwordsworth.com/the-paradox-of-the-proof/"}> web
              )
          %h3#another_alleged_proof Another alleged proof
          %p Kirti Joshi has put out an alleged proof of the abc conjecture on the arXiv:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kirti+Joshi"}> Kirti Joshi
              ,
              %em Construction of Arithmetic Teichmuller Spaces IV: Proof of the abc-conjecture
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.10430"}> arXiv:2403.10430
              )
          %p
            According to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"} Peter Scholze
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"}> Peter Scholze
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Global character of ABC/Szpiro inequalities
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/q/467995"} MathOverflow (version: 2024-03-29)
